      ],
      "extract": "The object in this paper is the symmetry group of a four-dimensional regular polytope, the 120-cell or its dual, the 600-cell. We note that this is among a general class of groups, so called, the Coxeter groups. They appear naturally in geometry and algebra. In 1935, the finite Coxeter groups were classified by Coxeter in terms of Coxeter-Dynkin diagrams [@Coxeter35]. Contrary to the crystallographic Coxeter groups,",
      "extractCut": true
